\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces (a) A sketch of the effect a gravitational wave has on a Michelson interferomter. The positions of the test masses, ETMX and ETMY, at different times during the gravitational wave's period is shown by the blue and pink mirrors. The electric fields within the interferometer are indicated: $\sqrt  {I_0}$ is the laser's amplitude and $\phi _x$ and $\phi _y$ is the phase acquired by the light as it travels up and down the arm. (b) To reach the sensitivity needed to detect gravitational wave, several mirrors need to be added to the basic Michelson interferometer: ITMX and ITMY boost the signal generated in the arms, the PRM increases the power at the beam splitter, and the SRM can be used to shape the quantum noise of the interferometer.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.10}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchaptersimplelayout}{{1.1}{10}{(a) A sketch of the effect a gravitational wave has on a Michelson interferomter. The positions of the test masses, ETMX and ETMY, at different times during the gravitational wave's period is shown by the blue and pink mirrors. The electric fields within the interferometer are indicated: $\sqrt {I_0}$ is the laser's amplitude and $\phi _x$ and $\phi _y$ is the phase acquired by the light as it travels up and down the arm. (b) To reach the sensitivity needed to detect gravitational wave, several mirrors need to be added to the basic Michelson interferometer: ITMX and ITMY boost the signal generated in the arms, the PRM increases the power at the beam splitter, and the SRM can be used to shape the quantum noise of the interferometer.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
