\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Comparison between the phase of the light reflected from a mirror versus the phase of light reflected from a cavity. The blue line shows the phase of the light that is reflected by a Fabry-Perot cavity. Initially, the cavity is on anti-resonance so perfect destructive interference occurs as the round-trip phase is 180\si {\degree }. The end mirror (ETM) is moved 90\si {\degree }, resulting in an extra 180\si {\degree } round-trip phase, and so the cavity resonates. The phase of the light reflected from the cavity rapidly changes as it moves through the resonance. The ETM is moved further, the cavity stops being resonant and the phase of the light is not changed as its reflected by the cavity. The orange line shows the phase of the light when a mirror is used instead of a cavity. The interferometers sensitivity is proportional to the slop $\frac  {d \phi _{\rm  {EM}}}{d \phi _{\rm  {ETM}}}$, and so by inserting another mirror in the arm near the beam splitter, a Fabry-Perot cavity is formed and the sensitivity of the interferometer is increased.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.13}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchapterfabryvsmirror}{{1.2}{13}{Comparison between the phase of the light reflected from a mirror versus the phase of light reflected from a cavity. The blue line shows the phase of the light that is reflected by a Fabry-Perot cavity. Initially, the cavity is on anti-resonance so perfect destructive interference occurs as the round-trip phase is 180\si {\degree }. The end mirror (ETM) is moved 90\si {\degree }, resulting in an extra 180\si {\degree } round-trip phase, and so the cavity resonates. The phase of the light reflected from the cavity rapidly changes as it moves through the resonance. The ETM is moved further, the cavity stops being resonant and the phase of the light is not changed as its reflected by the cavity. The orange line shows the phase of the light when a mirror is used instead of a cavity. The interferometers sensitivity is proportional to the slop $\frac {d \phi _{\rm {EM}}}{d \phi _{\rm {ETM}}}$, and so by inserting another mirror in the arm near the beam splitter, a Fabry-Perot cavity is formed and the sensitivity of the interferometer is increased.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
